// QFT4.QS0 - the four-qubit quantum Fourier transform.
//
// Demonstrates: parametric rotations (RZ/RY/RX), the controlled-phase gate
// (CPHASE), SWAP, and how a quantum algorithm encodes phase information.
//
// Qubit-level explanation:
//   We start from |0001> (X 0 sets qubit 0). The QFT maps a computational
//   basis state |k> to a uniform superposition whose relative phases encode
//   k:
//       |k>  ->  (1/sqrt(16)) * sum_j exp(2*pi*i*j*k/16) |j>.
//   The phases do not affect the measurement probabilities, so the result is
//   a roughly uniform distribution over all 16 basis states.
//
//   The circuit: for each qubit i, apply H i, then a controlled phase
//   CPHASE(i, j, pi/2^(j-i)) between i and every later qubit j. The final
//   SWAP ladder reverses the qubit order (standard QFT bit reversal), so the
//   measured state is the transform in the natural ordering.
//
//   Each angle is written out as a decimal; Plasma stores it in its constant
//   pool and applies the rotation via its deterministic CORDIC sin/cos.
//
// Run:
//   plasma run examples/QFT4.QS0 --shots 2000
//   plasma run examples/QFT4.QS0 --gpu --shots 2000
QUBITS 4
X 0
H 0
CPHASE 0 1 1.5707963267948966
CPHASE 0 2 0.7853981633974483
CPHASE 0 3 0.39269908169872414
H 1
CPHASE 1 2 1.5707963267948966
CPHASE 1 3 0.7853981633974483
H 2
CPHASE 2 3 1.5707963267948966
H 3
SWAP 0 3
SWAP 1 2
SHOT 2000
PRINT
END